<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Engine wiring, the vertical slices, and the fluent builder (design 01
    section 3.1, ADR-0065's ratified name).

    THIS PROJECT TAKES ITS FRAMEWORK TYPES FROM A FrameworkReference AND NOT
    FROM PACKAGES, and that is a choice with a measured reason rather than a
    habit. `AddNamiIdentity` needs `IServiceCollection`, `IValidateOptions<T>`,
    `ValidateOnStart()` and `BindConfiguration()`. In a plain `Microsoft.NET.Sdk`
    library on net10.0 every one of those arrives as a separate NuGet package,
    which would mean four PackageVersion rows and four licence reads owed under
    ADR-0026 before a single line of Nami's own logic exists. Referencing the
    ASP.NET Core shared framework instead brings them with the runtime, which
    design 01's library table already records as ".NET and ASP.NET Core, Runtime
    and web host, MIT, ADR-0030". The engine does the same thing: read at the
    nuget.org flat container on 2026-08-08, `OpenIddict.Server.AspNetCore`
    7.5.0's nuspec declares a frameworkReferences group for net8.0, net9.0 and
    net10.0 and carries one package dependency, `OpenIddict.Server`.

    THE DEPENDENCY RULE STILL HOLDS AND IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS. Design 01
    section 3.1 says `Core` depends on "`Abstractions` plus the protocol
    engine" and "must not reference any adapter, database provider, or cloud
    SDK". A shared framework is none of those three. The architecture suite
    asserts the real rule against the compiled assembly, not against this file,
    for the reason `tests/CLAUDE.md` records: a test that reads a project file
    fails on a correct project.

    THE ENGINE REFERENCE LANDED 2026-08-08 and the item group below carries it,
    with the reasoning in the comment beside it. Until then this block said there
    was none, deliberately, and the sentence it ended on is the one that survived
    contact with a measurement: "an unused reference is invisible to BOTH
    architecture facts ... adding the reference with the wiring that uses it is
    what makes the rule able to see it". The reference is now here and the wiring
    is not, so both facts are still inert. That was measured rather than assumed.

    TargetFrameworks reads the knob rather than naming a framework, because
    ADR-0030 parameter B multi-targets libraries and single-targets the host.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>$(NamiLibraryTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Nami.Identity.Abstractions/Nami.Identity.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE ENGINE, TWO PACKAGES OF THE EIGHT AND NOT THE OTHER SIX. Seed S-007
    settled that the engine is the eight identifiers design 04 section 6
    enumerates, which are exactly the eight `Directory.Packages.props` pins, and
    that the umbrella `OpenIddict.AspNetCore` is not one of them. Seed S-008
    settled which of the eight THIS assembly takes: `OpenIddict.Server` and its
    ASP.NET Core pass-through integration, and nothing else.

    No `Version` attribute, on purpose. Central Package Management fixes the
    version in `Directory.Packages.props`, and a version here is NU1008.

    WHY NOT THE OTHER SIX, each for its own reason rather than one rule.
    `.EntityFrameworkCore` is persistence and `.Quartz` is scheduling, and
    design 01 section 3.1 forbids `Core` from referencing an adapter or a
    database provider. `OpenIddict.Core` carries the managers AND the stores, so
    whether it belongs here is the question seed S-009 exists to answer, not one
    to pre-empt from a csproj. The three `.Validation` packages are listed by
    design 04 section 6 for the protocol host, and they arrive when something
    validates a token, which is seed S-010.

    THE TWO REFLECTION FACTS ARE STILL INERT, and adding this reference did NOT
    change that. Measured 2026-08-08 by adding the reference, building, and
    reading the emitted metadata: the assembly carries no `OpenIddict` string at
    all. An unused reference is elided, exactly as `tests/CLAUDE.md` recorded on
    2026-08-02. So the earlier plan for this seed, that the facts would gain
    something to catch here, was wrong and is corrected in `docs/SEEDS.md`. The
    facts go live when code touches an engine type, which is seed S-010.

    WHAT DID CHANGE IS THE RESTORE GRAPH, and that is this seed's real
    deliverable. It went from two nodes to ten, and five of the eight new ones
    are not OpenIddict at all. `docs/DEPENDENCY-LICENSES.md` carries the
    enumeration with every licence read at its own nuspec.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="OpenIddict.Server" />
    <PackageReference Include="OpenIddict.Server.AspNetCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
